<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:system="clr-namespace:System;assembly=System.Runtime">

    <!--
        HOW BIG A LETTER IS, HOW HEAVY, AND IN WHAT FACE. Values only, and not one style.

        THE FOURTH VALUE FILE, ON 2026-09-01, AND IT WAS PREDICTED BEFORE IT WAS NEEDED. Backlog 271
        was written that evening saying Values.xaml stood six lines under the markup ceiling and that
        the seam was already found - this one. The next change to that file hit the ceiling within
        the hour, which is a ratchet doing exactly what SizeCeilings says a ratchet is for.

        WHY THIS IS THE HONEST CUT AND NOT THE CONVENIENT ONE. The other value files each answer
        one question a person arrives with: Colours.xaml answers "what colour", Columns.xaml
        answers "how wide is that column", Values.xaml answers "how big is that thing and what
        shape is that mark", and since 2026-09-15 Spacing.xaml answers "how much air is around
        it". Type was the one subject left inside Values.xaml that nobody would look for under the
        word "values" - and it is the block every appearance argument in this product ends up
        citing, because two floors from Microsoft's guidance live in it.

        THE TWO FLOORS ARE THE WHOLE REASON THIS BLOCK IS ARGUED RATHER THAN LISTED: 12 for plain
        text and 14 for semibold, because a heavier stroke at a small size fills its own counters in.
        TypeScaleGuards holds both, so a size added here that breaks either fails the build.

        MERGED AMONG THE VALUE FILES, BEFORE EVERY STYLE FILE. It depends on nothing, and Text.xaml
        reaches five of these names through StaticResource, which resolves WHILE THAT FILE IS BEING
        READ - so this has to be in before it. App.xaml carries the order and three other places
        repeat it: WpfHost in the tests, tools/wpfui-probe, and the pool in AppearanceGuards.
    -->

    <!--
        Type. Three sizes here and a fourth below, which arrived with the reason this line used
        to say nobody had.

        TextSizeSmall WAS 11 UNTIL 2026-08-05 AND 11 IS BELOW THE FLOOR. Microsoft's typography
        guidance puts the minimum at 12 plain and 14 semibold, and the smallest size in this
        window carried the count line, the notice and the query error - three of the four
        sentences the window ever says. TypeScaleGuards holds the floor now, so it cannot go
        back down without somebody arguing with a test.

        TextSizeNormal stays at 13 and that is a decision rather than an oversight. The same
        guidance calls Body 14, but 13 is above the floor and the owner has twice bought this
        list's density with a measurement - their row spacing cost about 17% of the rows on
        screen. A window whose job is 810 services spends nothing on a point of type it does
        not need.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="TextSizeNormal">13</system:Double>
    <system:Double x:Key="TextSizeHeading">18</system:Double>
    <system:Double x:Key="TextSizeSmall">12</system:Double>

    <!--
        THE FIFTH SIZE, FOR EXACTLY ONE THING: the number on the machine overview, where the finding
        IS the number. At TextSizeHeading it was the same 18 as that screen's own title, so the two
        competed - photographed 2026-09-01, and OverviewView.xaml carries what the photograph showed.

        Thirty-two rather than the ladder's 28 Title, because this is a value rather than a title and
        Display is where the guidance puts one. TypeScaleGuards holds floors, not a ceiling.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="TextSizeDisplay">32</system:Double>

    <!--
        The fourth size, and the reason the comment above asked for. The floor for SEMIBOLD text
        is 14, not 12 - a heavier stroke at a small size fills its own counters in - so a column
        heading set semibold at TextSizeNormal would break the rule the same line of guidance
        sets. It also earns its keep twice: a heading one point larger than its data is the
        hierarchy this window had none of.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="TextSizeHeader">14</system:Double>

    <!--
        Semibold, and only for column headers. Bold is not on the ladder - the guidance says
        semibold instead of bold, and never italic, "because it reduces readability, especially
        for dyslexia". A header that looks like data is complaint 4 of the eleven.
    -->
    <FontWeight x:Key="TextWeightHeader">SemiBold</FontWeight>
    <FontWeight x:Key="TextWeightNormal">Normal</FontWeight>

    <FontFamily x:Key="TextFace">Segoe UI</FontFamily>

    <!--
        Monospaced, and only where alignment carries meaning: a launch path, a hash, a
        security descriptor. Reading a hash in a proportional face is how two different
        hashes look the same.
    -->
    <FontFamily x:Key="TextFaceFixed">Consolas</FontFamily>
</ResourceDictionary>
